Let \[F=\text{High Fidelity}, \qquad S=\text{High Selectivity}.\] Given, \[P(F)=0.81\] and \[P(F\cap S)=0.18.\] We need to find \[P(S|F).\] Using the conditional probability formula, \[P(S|F)=\frac{P(F\cap S)}{P(F)}\] \[=\frac{0.18}{0.81}\] \[=\frac{18}{81}
=\frac{2}{9}
\approx 0.222.\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }0.222}\]

Given, <div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td><strong>\(t\) in s</strong></td><td><strong>\(i(t)\) in A</strong></td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0.25</td><td>0.197</td></tr><tr><td>0.5</td><td>0.316</td></tr><tr><td>0.75</td><td>0.388</td></tr><tr><td>1.0</td><td>0.432</td></tr><tr><td>\(\vdots\)</td><td>\(\vdots\)</td></tr><tr><td>\(\infty\)</td><td>0.5</td></tr></table></div> According to the question, <img src="img/6673c1e34395976e.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> The current through the inductor is \[i(t)=i(\infty)+\big[i(0^+)-i(\infty)\big]e^{-t/\tau}
\tag{i}\] <strong>(i) At \(t=0^{-}\) / steady state</strong><br/>In steady state, the inductor behaves as a short circuit. <img src="img/169b9a56b7c0901a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> \[i_L(0^-)=0~\text{A}\] From the property of inductor, \[i_L(0^-)=i_L(0^+)=0~\text{A}\] <strong>(ii) At \(t&gt;0\) (transient)</strong><br/>For an RL network, \[\tau=\frac{L}{R}\] <strong>(iii) At \(t=\infty\) / steady state</strong><br/>The inductor behaves as a short circuit. <img src="img/24f7b4a6ada2605b.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,192px)"> \[i(\infty)=\frac{1}{R}\ \text{A}\] Substituting \(i(\infty)\), \(i(0^+)\) and \(\tau\) into (i), \[i(t)
=
\frac{1}{R}
-\frac{e^{-t/\tau}}{R}
=
\frac{1-e^{-Rt/L}}{R}\] From the table, \[i(\infty)=\frac{1}{R}=0.5\] \[R=2~\Omega\] Also, \[i(0.25)=0.197\] \[0.197
=
\frac{1-e^{-\frac{2}{L}(0.25)}}{2}\] Solving, \[L=1~\text{H}\] Hence, \[\boxed{1}\]

The non-inverting terminal of the op-amp is grounded, hence the inverting terminal is at virtual ground. The multiplier has a scaling factor of \(1\), therefore its output is \[V_{m}=V_{2}V_{o}.\] Applying KCL at the inverting node, \[\frac{V_{1}-0}{R}+\frac{V_{m}-0}{R}=0.\] Substituting \(V_{m}=V_{2}V_{o}\), \[V_{1}+V_{2}V_{o}=0.\] Hence, \[V_{o}=-\frac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}
      =-\frac{15}{3}
      =-5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{-5}\]

Since the non-inverting terminal is grounded, \[V_{+}=0 \;\Rightarrow\; V_{-}\approx 0\] (virtual ground). For the inverting amplifier, \[V_{o}=-\frac{R_{f}}{R_{in}}V_{in}
=-\frac{2\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}\times 10\,\text{V}
=-20\,\text{V}.\] However, the op-amp is powered by \[\pm 15\,\text{V},\] so the output cannot go below \(-15\,\text{V}\). Hence the output saturates at the negative supply rail. \[\boxed{\text{(b) }-15\,\text{V}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not mark \(-20\,\text{V}\) as the answer without checking the supply limits.</li></ul></div>

The Zener-diode network limits the output voltage to \[V_{o}=\pm 7\,\text{V}.\] <img src="img/da03e941fcbf06da.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Let the voltage at the non-inverting terminal be \(V_{+}\). Applying KCL at this node, \[\frac{V_{+}-V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{V_{+}-2}{0.5\,\text{k}\Omega}=0.\] Hence, \[V_{+}
=\frac{\dfrac{V_{o}}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}+\dfrac{2}{0.5\,\text{k}\Omega}}
{\dfrac{1}{10\,\text{k}\Omega}+\dfrac{1}{0.5\,\text{k}\Omega}}
=\frac{V_{o}+40}{21}.\] For the two output states, \[V_{UTP}=\frac{7+40}{21}
=\frac{47}{21}\,\text{V},\] \[V_{LTP}=\frac{-7+40}{21}
=\frac{33}{21}\,\text{V}.\] Therefore, the hysteresis width is \[V_{H}=V_{UTP}-V_{LTP}
=\frac{47-33}{21}
=\frac{14}{21}
=\frac{2}{3}\,\text{V}
\approx 0.67\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{0.67}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The Zener diodes clamp the output to \(\pm7\,\text{V}\).</li><li>Hysteresis width is \(V_{UTP}-V_{LTP}\).</li></ul></div>

The op-amp is configured as a non-inverting amplifier with \[A_v=1+\frac{R_f}{R_1}
=1+\frac{1\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=2.\] The input to the non-inverting terminal is obtained through an RC low-pass filter: \[\frac{V_{+}}{V_{in}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+(\omega RC)^2}},\] where \[R=10\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
C=10\,\text{nF}.\] Hence, \[\left|\frac{V_o}{V_{in}}\right|
=\frac{2}{\sqrt{1+(\omega RC)^2}}.\] Given, \[|V_o|=0.95\,|V_{in}|,\] therefore, \[\frac{2}{\sqrt{1+(\omega RC)^2}}=0.95.\] Solving, \[1+(\omega RC)^2=\left(\frac{2}{0.95}\right)^2,\] \[\omega RC=\sqrt{\left(\frac{2}{0.95}\right)^2-1}
\approx1.857.\] Since \[RC=(10\,\text{k}\Omega)(10\,\text{nF})
=10^{-4}\,\text{s},\] \[\omega=\frac{1.857}{10^{-4}}
=1.857\times10^4\,\text{rad/s}.\] Therefore, \[f=\frac{\omega}{2\pi}
=\frac{1.857\times10^4}{2\pi}
\approx2.956\times10^3\,\text{Hz}
\approx2.96\,\text{kHz}.\] \[\boxed{2.96}\]

Four seats around a square table: North, East, South, West. Given constraints: <ul><li>Rahul is to the left of Murali.</li><li>Srinivas is to the right of Arul.</li></ul> Fix Murali at East \(\Rightarrow\) Rahul at North (left of Murali). Remaining seats South and West for Srinivas and Arul. Srinivas to the right of Arul: \[\text{Arul = South} \Rightarrow \text{right = West} \Rightarrow \text{Srinivas = West}\ \checkmark\] Final arrangement: \[\text{North = Rahul},\quad \text{East = Murali},\quad \text{South = Arul},\quad \text{West = Srinivas}\] <img src="img/d30cf094c033b293.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,269px)"> Opposite pairs: \[\textcolor{red}{\text{Rahul (N)} \longleftrightarrow \text{Arul (S)}}
\qquad
\textcolor{blue}{\text{Srinivas (W)} \longleftrightarrow \text{Murali (E)}}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) Srinivas and Murali}}\]

The slant height of the cone is the distance from the apex to the base edge. Using the given base radius \(r = 5\,\text{cm}\) and height \(h = 12\,\text{cm}\): \[l = \sqrt{r^{2} + h^{2}}
      = \sqrt{5^{2} + 12^{2}}
      = \sqrt{25 + 144}
      = \sqrt{169}
      = 13\,\text{cm}\] When the cone rolls on the ground with point P fixed, the apex traces a circle of radius \(l = 13\,\text{cm}\). The cone returns to its original position after the base circle (circumference \(2\pi r = 10\pi\)) has rolled out completely. Number of rotations of the cone's axis: \[n = \frac{2\pi l}{2\pi r} = \frac{l}{r} = \frac{13}{5}\] Angle swept by the cone (in radians): \[\theta = n \times 2\pi
           = \frac{13}{5} \times 2\pi
           = \frac{26\pi}{5}\] The question asks for the angle the cone travels about P (the swept angle at the apex). The base circumference \(= 2\pi r = 10\pi\) must equal the arc length swept: \[\text{Arc length} = l \times \theta
    \implies 10\pi = 13\,\theta
    \implies \theta = \frac{10\pi}{13}\] \[\boxed{ \text D) \dfrac{10\pi}{13}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Slant height \(l = \sqrt{r^{2} + h^{2}}\).</li><li>When a cone rolls without slipping, the arc swept on the ground equals the base circumference: \(l\,\theta = 2\pi r\).</li><li>Angle travelled \(\theta = \dfrac{2\pi r}{l}\).</li></ul></div>

Evaluate \(\dfrac{(x+y) - |x-y|}{2}\). <strong>Case 1:</strong> \(x \geq y \Rightarrow |x-y| = x-y\): \[\frac{(x+y)-(x-y)}{2}
    = \frac{2y}{2}
    = y
    = \min(x,y)\] <strong>Case 2:</strong> \(x &lt; y \Rightarrow |x-y| = y-x\): \[\frac{(x+y)-(y-x)}{2}
    = \frac{2x}{2}
    = x
    = \min(x,y)\] In both cases the expression equals \(\min(x,y)\). \[\boxed{\text{B. the minimum of }x\text{ and }y}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Standard identity: \(\min(x,y) = \dfrac{(x+y)-|x-y|}{2}\).</li><li>Similarly: \(\max(x,y) = \dfrac{(x+y)+|x-y|}{2}\).</li></ul></div>

\(X\) is a 30-digit number starting with digit 4 followed by the digit 7. <strong>Number of digits in \(X^{3}\):</strong><br/>The number of digits in any integer \(N\) is \(\lfloor \log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\). \[\text{Digits in } X^{3}
    = \lfloor 3\log_{10} X \rfloor + 1\] Since \(X\) is a 30-digit number: \[10^{29} \leq X &lt; 10^{30}\] \[29 \leq \log_{10} X &lt; 30\] \[87 \leq 3\log_{10} X &lt; 90\] More precisely, \(X\) starts with \(47\ldots\): \[\log_{10} X \approx 29 + \log_{10}(4.7\ldots)
    \approx 29 + 0.672 = 29.672\] \[3\log_{10} X \approx 3 \times 29.672 = 89.016\] \[\text{Digits in } X^{3}
    = \lfloor 89.016 \rfloor + 1
    = 89 + 1
    = 90\] \[\boxed{\text{A) 90 digits}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Number of digits in \(N\) \(= \lfloor \log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\).</li><li>For \(N^{k}\): digits \(= \lfloor k\log_{10} N \rfloor + 1\).</li><li>Use the leading digits to estimate \(\log_{10} X\) precisely.</li></ul></div>

Calculate the total standalone workload for all jobs: <ul><li>Job P: \(2 \times 30 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job Q: \(3 \times 20 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job R: \(1 \times 60 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li><li>Job S: \(4 \times 15 = 60\,\text{min}\)</li></ul> \[\text{Total Workload} = 240\,\text{min}\] With 2 parallel identical machines: \[\text{Minimum Time}
    = \frac{240}{2} = 120\,\text{min}
    = 2\,\text{hours}\] Optimal allocation: <ul><li><strong>Machine 1:</strong> \(\text{R}+\text{P} = 60+60
          = 120\,\text{min}\)</li><li><strong>Machine 2:</strong> \(\text{Q}+\text{S} = 60+60
          = 120\,\text{min}\)</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{A) }2\,\text{hours}}\]
